   "notes": "Extends the Anatolian/Egypt entries with Coele-Syria (Ptolemaic from about 301, lost 200; Coele-Syria in the Polybian sense = south of the Eleutherus, the Nahr al-Kabir, which is where Wikipedia's account of Seleucid 'Coele Syria and Phoenicia' puts the old Ptolemaic frontier). The northern edge is drawn at 34.6 N. Damascus is the least secure claim: Ptolemaic in the 3rd century on the Zenon-era evidence I recall but not verified in this pass; it is a separate ring so the lead can drop it and let the Seleucid default take it. Tyre, Sidon, Byblos and Berytus are Ptolemaic with civic autonomy (Philocles, admiral and 'king of the Sidonians', served Ptolemy II). Transjordan (Ammanitis, Gilead, Bashan/Hauran) is in; Edom and the Hisma are Nabataean in the Egypt/Arabia file. Judea sits earlier in the list and is cut out.",
